--->>25. Explain state locking in Terraform — why is it important?
State locking prevents multiple users from modifying the same Terraform state at the same time.
It ensures that only one Terraform operation (plan/apply/destroy) runs against the infrastructure at once.

Why it matters

Avoids race conditions — two applies running together can create or delete conflicting resources.
Prevents corrupted state files — partial writes can break the entire environment.
Stops duplicate resources — like two identical VPCs or subnets being created.

--->>26. What is Terraform drift and what actions should we take?

Terraform drift happens when the real cloud infrastructure changes, but those changes are not in Terraform code or state — usually because someone modified resources manually in the console 

